lfortney@hamlet.umd.edu (Linda Fortney) writes:

>    I do hope your government decides that blanket censorship is not
> only monumentally unjust, but impossible to enforce.

        It has. Quite some time ago. In any case, where did you get
        the idea that McMaster University was the government? We know
        Americans generally know diddly about Canada, but this is
        stretching it.

        If you collect censorship stories, you should first find out
        what it is. Private discretion is not censorship. Does UMD's
        library have David Hamilton's books? No?! Is that censorship?
        It might be disappointing that the work of a man who inspired
        a lasting look in women's fashions, a hit song (Leonard
        Cohen's "Suzanne"), more than one photographer, a whole genre
        of film, and exposed the world to the wonderful music of
        Francis Lai should be kept from the shelves. Maybe it's
        because the pretty girls seem a little young; but it isn't
        censorship, it's merely anal-retentive. There are other places
        to find his work where people are less likely to take offense
        than in the average university library.

        Libraries are allowed to be anal-retentive and to choose their
        holdings. The first amendment constrains your government, not
        private individuals or institutions.

        Cheers,
                Marc

