In article <1994Apr1.110605.14296@mnemosyne.cs.du.edu>, jmaynard@nyx10.cs.du.edu
 (Jay Maynard) writes:
>Here's an opportunity for folks who claim to be against censorship to stand up
>and oppose it in all its forms, even when non-politically correct subjects are
>being censored. It looks like, from the admin's comments, that ads for other
>kinds of things are passed through uncensored...

Here's the situation:  All local bulletin boards at Lehigh are moderated.
This means that some individual is responsible for previewing each and every
posting before it is made publicly viewable.  Lehigh claims all rights as
publisher to determine the content of university funded information
services.  In particular, For Sale postings are treated as ads in any
newspaper, and Lehigh reserves sole right to accept or reject them.  As a
specific guideline, we restrict sale postings to "hassle-free" items.  For
that reason, we do not post t-shirt ads, ads for businesses, and a host of
other topics, all because of numerous problems and complaints in the past.
No judgement as to worthiness is made.  Also, all responsibility for the
determination of legality of postings is specifically disclaimed.

The ability to place ads on line is nothing more than a convenience.  It is
an ancillary function that is not in the slightest way related to the
educational mission of the university.  It is a personal service, not an
academic resource.  However, the service is here, people like it, and so we
continue to provide it.  This is clearly a non-essential service and content
should be dictated by the desires of the community at large, not by any
misplaced notions of academic freedom.
