Well, a joke that is not a chain letter might still be considered
"frivolous." However, if you are SO tight with resources that you 
are trying to prevent users from sending each other jokes, or 
personal e-mail, you are going to have a rough time and you will
make your users miserable.

There's *always* room for a *small* amount of levity in any working
environment. When I was younger, I worked at several factories. The biggest
was a no-nonsense time-clock-punching assembly-line sweatshop, rotten pay,
the sort of place where if you left your station to get a cup of coffee or
go to the bathroom your supervisor would take notice. 
  Even there, people used to use the copy machine to run off occasional
cartoons or jokes, which would circulate through the place. The bosses
knew, and tolerated it. These guys didn't care at all about psychology or
civil liberties; I think they realized that you have to allow people to 
have a little control or else you make the workplace TOO much like a jail.
If people feel that the "bosses" are the enemy, you won't get as much 
work done.

I don't understand why some schools try to limit the types of uses that
people put their computers to (beyond stopping illegal activity.)  We use
quotas here and it's a LOT easier on everyone. You get a meg in your home
directory, that is yours to do what you like. You get a LOT of space in
your course directory (on another disk) and keep that for course work. We
ask users not to do intensive stuff during prime time unless it is for
courses, and we send nasty mail to everyone over quota when the disk fills
up, and to everyone we see using ftp and telnet or IRC or games when the
CPU starts to bog down. We've also got some automated security software
that checks for breakins and account sharing, and a cleanup script that
runs around and deletes *.o and *.dvi and core and other "junk" files.

The bottom line is, you can run a system and just check for *problems.*
You can let people goof around if they aren't hurting anyone. 

