Actually, according to the Supreme Court radio and TV are not full
public forums. In _Red Lion_ the Supreme Court said that because of
unique spectrum scarcity it was legal for the FCC to regulate the
content of broadcasts in was that would be illegal for a public forum.
In _FCC v. Pacifica_, the Supreme Court said that the FCC could
regulate "dirty" words on Radio and TV because of broadcast's scarcity
and "unique intrusiveness". Note, however, the FCC can't ban "dirty"
words, only channel them to later hours. Also, Netnews does not depend
on a scarce spectrum and it is not intrusive (since you can easily
skip or kill Mr. Kohr's articles.). It is more like cable and in "Slip
Opinion, 1986:6", the Court said that cable was proteced by the First
Amendment and could not be content regulated like broadcast TV.

