Wrong wrong wrong.  The FCC issues citations for violating the terms of a
license (contract) for public broadcasters.  It's a "time and place" 
restriction.  And the FCC just recently got their hand slapped by the Supreme
Court for going overboard with their "indecency" restrictions.  The authority
of the FCC in regulating broadcasters is a limited authority created because
Congress believed that the airwaves are a (very limited) public resource.
Content rules are not allowed in wire communications--except for content that
would be just as illegal on some other medium.

]Stop trying to be an armchair lawyer, Carl. Don't you have homework to 
]do?

Please get a life!  You've repeatedly demonstrated you don't know what you're
talking about, whether search warrants or the FCC.  Carl probably reads more
court citations every day than you've seen in your life.  If you do see them
on a regular basis, they sure as heck don't sink in very well.            

