Yes.  It's a set of computer files containing personal data, which you
can search using a software tool.

C'mon now -- haven't you ever found somebody's phone number by
grepping for his .signature in a newsgroup?  I have.

The only problem is that Usenet seems to blow the minds of anyone in
the Data Protection Registrar's office whom you actually talk to.

"So, this database, how big is it?"

"About 100MB larger every day.  Of course, we delete stuff from the 
database all the time; anything more than a few days old usually gets 
deleted automatically."

"Er... ok, who's entering this data?"

"I dunno.  Anyone can.  I have no way of telling who."

"Errrmm...  Well, all right then, where do they put the data in?"

"Usually somewhere on planet earth at the moment, though potentially
Shuttle astronauts could add stuff.  I have no way of knowing more precisely
where, though."

"Errrm...  Well, what sort of data is being put in this database?"

"Anything people decide to put in.  I've seen names and addresses,
photographs, recordings of people's voices, information about people's
sexual preferences, how they vote, what they earn, what cars they
drive, you name it.  I have no way of knowing what information someone 
may choose to add tomorrow.  Usually people only send out information
about themselves, but of course that makes no difference according to
the act."

"OK, so how do you retrieve the data?"

"Well, let's say I want Ken Johnson's phone number.  I happen to know
that he posts to uk.misc a lot, so I type cd /export/news/uk/misc,
grep Johnson .overview, and cat one of the indicated files, and
there's his name and address and telephone number in full at the
bottom."

"So, there's a database of unknown size, containing potentially
any kind of personal information, accessible for update by an unknown
number of total strangers in unknown locations all over the world, and
you have no idea what's in it until you look."

"That's about the size of it, yes.  And you don't seem to have a tick-box
on the form for that kind of thing."


mathew

