If the Homolka trial had been open to the public, the truth would have 
come out a long time ago.  In this environment, any juror will have in 
the back of his or her mind all the rumours.  Even if a juror did take an 
oath to weigh the evidence, s/he'd wonder if the state was hiding 
something, notwithstanding any full disclousre rules.

>The Rodney King incident shows quite adequately how an "absolutely open
>and free press" can trample over the rights of an accused person.  All
