subject to review.  These messages have existed since 1985.  Guidelines
evolve based on our ability to administratively handle the load generated
both by postings and complaints about postings (the latter being the heavier
load, thus driving the guidelines).  Since these are non-essential services,
our choice is to either minimize the headache or eliminate the services.

>I am aware of the current disk space problems.  (I am also aware of
>the reasons for the current state of affairs).  My problems stem with
>the conflicting use policies, the original reasons for the decision,
>and the 'Lehigh Knows Best' attitude that seems to permeate the entire
>administration, including the Computing Center administration.

The initial usenet news policy approved by the CCAC stated that the facility
would be offered on an experimental basis.  If operational or other problems
arose, it was subject to termination.  That status still applies.  Alt groups
were specifically addressed: in that they are by definition not sanctioned by
the usenet community at large, they would not be carried.  This was a simple
and perfectly reasonable evaluation criterion.  The policy did allow
individual Alt groups to be requested by faculty for use in courses.  Since
then, the subject has been re-examined a number of times and the CCAC has
maintained that the cost to support the extra load could not be justified.
