In article <2nrhu3$1dh@nyheter.chalmers.se>, d9bertil@dtek.chalmers.se (Bertil Jonell) writes:
>   In Sweden we have a government agency called 'Datainspektionen' which
> can be translated as the 'computer oversight commission'. It's purpose
> is to see that the special computer laws are followed, and issue several
> types of permits that you need to do specific things.
>   The main thing is that it isn't allowed to have so-called 'personal registry'
> unless you get a permit. Personal registry is the legal word that is defined
> something like 'a computerized system where information on individuals
> is filed'. 'Information on individuals' include their name, and especially
> their picture, and very especially their political opinions.

I prefer calling it "the Data Inquisition".  Just like the catholic
inquisition banned books and required the authors to confess their
lies, the DI has the power to forbid a register with personal info if
its character isn't in line with the law, and to require maintainers
to make any corrections necessary to get the register up to date with
reality.

The Swedish Data Act was established in 1973, and has undergone only
partial revision since.  It was written in response to public fear that
large registers on people might be misused for invasion of privacy
(say, by automatically comparing wages and salaries with taxes paid
in order to identify potential dodgers among the general public).
In addition to regulating personal registers, the Data Act contains
a paragraph prohibiting unauthorized access to computer records in
general.

Contrary to what Bertil suggests, pictures of people aren't specifically
targeted by the present law, because in 1973 it wasn't technically
possible to digitally store or process photographic images (according to
an analysis of the history of the law, published in 1993 by a commission
working on a new Data Act).  The problem of invasion of privacy by
storing images of people on digital media (so called Photo CD) is still
being investigated by the commission.  In 1992, Posten Bildbanken AB
received permission from the DI for their commercial image database, but
the permit came with a number of restrictions, and they appealed against
the decision.

In the beginning, the DI was indeed pretty powerful, and it used its
power to prevent certain questionable registers, which the authorities
were planning, from being established.  The government was concerned
that the DI actually prevented the creation of various registers which
were essential (or at least very useful) to the authorities, and soon
asked the parliament to amend the law, so that the government (or the
parliament) would be able to override the decisions of the DI, when
needed.  The parliament agreed to amending the law.  Now the DI could
concentrate on making sure that the general public (of which only the
major corporations used computers to any significant degree) complied
with the law, rather than interfering with public administration.

When the director general of the DI, Jan Freese, complained that the DI
had lost the power it was originally intended to have, he was awarded a
new position in a different office, an offer he couldn't refuse.

>   If you want to set up such a registry, you have to apply for a permit,
> and the Computer Oversight Commission will then judge if they find its
> purpose essential enough to warrant creation. That is not bureaucratic
> overkill, that judgement is what the law expressively tells them to do.

To be more specific, the law allows for three different levels of
permission.  First, a register which is created and maintained for
strictly private purposes (such as your friends' phone numbers in
your computer notebook, or your family research database) doesn't
require any formal application at all.  Simply create and use it.

Second, if your register is for other than strictly private purposes,
such as a database on customers or employees, or users of your BBS,
or any personal register maintained as part of your work, you need a
license from the DI.  This license is tied to you as the owner of the
register (the owner can be either a physical or a legal body).  You
can have as many registers or databases as you like, as soon as you
have this license.  I believe there is a fee connected with the
license, but there are no formal requirements for getting one, other
than providing your name and address (the purpose of this is so that
the DI can keep track on who maintain personal registers).

Third, if you want to gather records on people with whom you have no
obvious connection (such as a research database on the shopping habits
of retired women, or a biographical reference database on 19th-century
clergy), or if you want to record sensitive information (about their
health, history of crime, political opinions and similar things), you
also have to apply to the DI for a permission for each such register
specifically, and that permission can be denied to you if the DI thinks
your register may threaten somebody's privacy.

>   This has led to some interesting consequences. First of all any sort of
> text file that includes somebodys name is a 'personal registry', so you'll
> need a permit for that. In practice however nobody does.

In practice, few people are aware of these consequences of the law.
The DI has issued some 50,000 licenses, but estimates that the real
figure (number of owners of personal registers requiring a license)
should be somewhere around 500,000 (population of Sweden: 8.6 million).

I believe most major companies and institutions (such as universities)
are pretty careful to obtain the necessary licenses and permissions.
To most people, the law is a bulwark against Big Brother, in the shape
of public authorities and large insurance corporations, invading the
privacy of individuals by keeping too close an eye on their incomes,
taxes, political opinions and whereabouts in general.  However, recent
media attention to some of the weird consequences may be changing that.

>   A person who's name I've temporarily forgotten who's been involved in 
> several debates on the subject decided to write a book about it. In that
> book he would naturally have to mention some real people, and also their
> views about protections on personal integrity, ie he wanted to set up 
> a personal registry on political opinions. He's also been pretty critical
> against the Computer Oversight Commission in the past.
>   So to put the spotlight on the law, he applied for a permit, and was 
> denied becaue they didn't feel that the book was essential enough to be
> written. And he asked the Computer Oversight Commission wether they really
> felt that they could impose prior restraint on a book (which is illegal
> under our 'constitution') and they answered something like "Well, that's 
> the law and we all gotta follow it."
>   He wrote the book without the permit in order to get sued, but they didn't
> bite.

His name is Anders R Olsson (oops, I don't have a license either :-).
The title of the book is "Yttrandefrihet och tryckfrihet" ("Freedom of
Expression and Freedom of Print"; unfortunately for most netters it's
only published in Swedish), and it's basically a legal handbook for
journalists.  Even simply creating his manuscript on his Macintosh
instead of on a mechanical typewriter appearantly meant that he was
legally required to obtain a license, maybe also a permit due to the
nature of some of the personal information he were to include.  However,
I think he applied for permission for the manuscript as well as for all
relevant research material he might want to keep on his Macintosh, as
one single "register".

At first, he didn't even get an answer from the DI.  So, he wrote the
book, and it was published in late 1992.  Still no reaction from the
DI.  He then filed a complaint against the DI with the Ombudsman
(the Swedish Parliamentary Commissioner for the Judiciary and Civil
Administration, "JO"), in order to get at least a reply.  Sometime
in August or September of 1993, the DI replied.  His application had
been rejected, which of course was in line with his intentions.  Now
I think he has filed another complaint against the DI, claiming they
are executing prior restraint with respect to his freedom to publish
books.  I don't know whether that has produced any formal response.

>   The second consequence is that every computer system that holds a password
> file or a list of authorised users is a personal registry which need a 
> permit from the Computer Oversight Commission which they can deny if they
> don't feel the purpose of the computer system is essential enough. That
> means everybodys systems: companies, universities, the rest of the government,
> the defence, every single little BBS. And in light of that the Director-
> General of the Computer Oversight Commission, Anitha Bondestam, have described
> modems as "Only usable for computer crime. As soon as kids get a modem they
> start to try to break in everywhere, never buy a modem to your kids, they are
> just trouble." one might have some idea how many permits for BBS'es she'd
> issue.

"We shouldn't encourage hackers", she also said according to those
who read that interview (I haven't).  To bad she wasn't around in
the seventies, when those evil hackers wroke havoc on society by
creating the personal computer industry in the first place. ;-)

Now, the DI is in fact well aware that this law, originally created in
1973 when computers were still in the hands of authorities and major
corporations only, is severely out of touch with reality, but they
have to follow it.  A proposal for a new and more liberal Data Act
(abolishing the license system, among other things) was presented in
early 1993, but for some reason (either rejection due to heavy criticism
for not being liberal enough, or cancellation of the process due to a
sudden government intent of waiting for input from the European Union)
the proposal was scrapped.  We are still waiting for the next proposal.

In order to minimize the terror in the meantime, the DI simply avoids
answering problematic questions, like they tried with the application
filed by Anders R Olsson.  However, I don't think they understand the
entire scope of the problem with respect to freedom of expression, and
some of Bondestam's statements are food for worry.  For instance, I
think it was she who considered it a problem that the algorithm for
computing the checksum of Swedish social security numbers had been
made public, making it possible for spoofers to create fake numbers.
Big deal.  That algorithm is dead trivial...

>   Nobody gives a flying about this law however, and while she rails in the 
> media the Computer Oversight Commission lacks any enforcement powers 
> whatsoever(1). They could theoretically order the police to investigate the
> crime but they haven't. Yet.

Anders R Olsson notes that one author has actually been fined for
not having appropriate permission for the personal information he
gathered on people in preparation for a book on neo-nazi groups
operating in Sweden (the book was published).  Keeping records on
people's political affiliations is a fishy thing, but should you
really prevent private individuals (as opposed to the state) from
doing it?

> They were allowed to keep backissues and newswires but access to them would
> have to be restricted to people with legitimate research interests. The
> Computer Oversight Commission decided that journalists had legitimate
> research interests and ordinary citizens had not. They said straight out that
> "No person should have to suffer from what they did five years ago." and
> on the question 'but what about politicians?' they answered "*Especially* not
> politicians!"

About a year ago, members of the Swedish parliament suggested making
parliamentary records (plenary protocols and such) available to the
public via dial-in modems (and, I presume, regular computer networks).
The DI quickly discouraged them from implementing such a thing, saying
it would threaten the privacy of the MPs by recording their political
opinions.  Now, the parliament may easily override the opinion of the
DI by deciding to establish this particular "personal register" (there
is explicit provision in the Data Act for this), but appearantly they
haven't done so.

The main Swedish telecom operator, Telia, has created a CD-ROM version
of the telephone directory, containing all Swedish subscribers on one
disc.  However, the DI doesn't allow them to sell this CD-ROM (that's
what Telia said when we phoned them about it), I guess maybe because
the law might technically require all buyers to obtain a permit for
owning their copy, or something in that direction...

Around 1987, somebody pointed out that the Swedish Radio newscast log,
which was computerized, contained information on people mentioned in
various news items, and was thus subject to the Data Act.  "In case
somebody who had been mentioned in the newscast log, be it Ronald
Reagan, Saddam Hussein, or Madonna, didn't want to have his or her
name searchable in the log, Swedish Radio might have been legally
required to erase that particular entry from it", Anders R Olsson
wrote.  The Data Act was quickly amended to give the DI the right to
grant exemptions from that requirement to certain register owners.

>   To prevent ordinary rabble from starting a fake paper in order to find
> out what the politicians would rather forget, the Computer Oversight
> Commission proposed setting up a list of authorised newspapers that were 
> allowed to have the right to research. Naturally the Computer Oversight 
> Commission would judge wether a paper was legit or not.

The recent proposal for a new law included an explicit exception for
computer files "for the direct technical use in the production of
printed material", such as a word processor manuscript.  However,
at least one of the members of the commission writing the proposal
voiced his fear that such an exception might be used by someone
actually publishing his register in a fake newspaper in order to
circumvent the law, and maintained that no exception should be made.

This shows that there is a clear conflict between the intent of the
Data Act (to regulate information processing) and freedom of print
(or freedom of expression in general).  Which side will win?  The
Freedom of Print Act has constitutional status in Sweden, and thus
overrides any ordinary law (such as the Data Act) in case of conflict.
However, this requires people to recognize the conflict in the first
place, and the Freedom of Print Act of today cannot be guaranteed to
cover anything but actually printed material.

Romania abolished the typewriter license requirement in 1990, after
the fall of Ceaucescu.  Hey, Joshua, wanna nuke another wall?  No, I
don't have time for tic-tac-toe (nor the launch code), sorry... ;-)

When the Data Act was introduced in 1973, Sweden was first in the
world (or at least so they say) to regulate the keeping of computer
records, aiming to protect personal privacy.  It seems several other
countries have followed the example, or established similar laws
independently of ours.  I have heard rumours that even the USA is
planning similar legislation.  Is this true?  Now, I'd recommend
you to pay attention to where that may be leading.  Take care.

I'm in the process of translating the Swedish Data Act into English,
as a warning example.  However, I can't spend much time on it, so
please don't rush me.  Maybe a translation is already available?
If another one doesn't turn up, I'll announce my translation in
the relevant places, so don't bug me for it in advance.  Thanks.
