>I have been told by the local postmaster that not just FAQs but *all*
>newgroup postings are compiled and are available on CD-ROM.  If this is
>true, is there a way to have this practice stopped, or at least get *my*
>postings deleted if I want?

No!  This is silly.  You don't have any control over who sees your
posts now.  This is why you (hopefully) get a message every time you
go to make a post which says "This post will be distributed across the
entire civilized world and will cost hundreds if not thousands of
dollars to send.  Are you sure you want to do this?"  At NO place does
it say that USENET is expressedly confined to machines talkking NNTP
to each other over TCP/IP.  If all USENET is is a wide-area protocol
for transmitting messages, why not include other transmission media,
like mag tape or CD-ROM?  Don't say "well, but, these guys are
CHARGING for this CD-ROM", because what's the difference between that
and some online service charging you by the hour or month for reading
USENET?  Or a service provider charging by the gigabyte for a USENET
feed for a smaller site?

And what did you have to say on a newsgroup that you wouldn't want
later said elsewhere?  It's still in the same context, remember.  If
you feel your words have real value, save them, compile them, make an
attractive package and market it.  But I just don't see any functional
or copyright-issue difference between USENET feeds and USENET CD's.

