A topic that comes up every so often somewhere on the net when someone 
notices that USENET is not just NNTP or UUCP anymore.

Summary of the last few times it came up: USENET goes where USENET goes,
and USENET is more than NNTP and UUCP, and has been for a very long
time. USENET on CD-ROM is a trivial extension of USENET on tapes or
USENET via satellite. Posters have granted permission for their articles
to be copied wherever USENET goes by the simple act of posting. They
have no control over which media their posting appears on, because there
is no USENET control mechanism for this.

In article <2o4fh1$rd1@vw-4.dtd.com>, Dave Walls <dwalls@rodent.dtd.com> wrote:
>
>The question is this:  if you publish something to 
>the internet,  does that give everyone permision to
>publish it to another medium? 

No, that is not the question. The question is about copyright on USENET.
The Internet is just one transport medium for USENET. If we were to
take your proposed question as being valid, the answer must be "yes",
because by the act of posting you have given permission for Internet,
UUCP, FIDO, WWIV and a host of other transport mechanisms.

