In article <2nrhu3$1dh@nyheter.chalmers.se>, Bertil Jonell <d9bertil@dtek.chalmers.se> wrote:

>  The main thing is that it isn't allowed to have so-called 'personal registry'
>unless you get a permit. 

Just in case anyone's interested, the text referred to is SFS
1992:446.

This is correct in general.  But there are a number of important
exceptions, which includes registers made by a private person for
personal use, registers placed in archives, certain medical and social
registers, and one or two other cases.

>Personal registry is the legal word that is defined
>something like 'a computerized system where information on individuals
>is filed'. 'Information on individuals' include their name, and especially
>their picture, and very especially their political opinions.

But ... the text also states (section 3) that "the Data Inspection
shall give permission to create and keep a personal register, if there
is no reason to believe that, considering the rules in sections 5 and
6, a violation of the personal integrity will occur."

(Section 5 and 6 just say that the Data Inspection may provide
regulations for the register, if there are other risks for invasion of
personal integrity.)

The focus is thus on ensuring the personal integrity of the persons
registred, not on the register itself.

As regards political opinion (or religious or equivalent opinion) the
law text does allow political (or religious or equivalent)
organizations to register any political (or ...) beliefs, since this
belief is the basis for the organization (and thus, I assume, not a
case where personal integrity is involved.)

>>  The second consequence is that every computer system that holds a password
>>file or a list of authorised users is a personal registry which need a 
>>permit from the Computer Oversight Commission which they can deny if they
>>don't feel the purpose of the computer system is essential enough. 

'Essence' is not important - personal integrity is.  I am fairly
certain I've seen a note that registers with public information (name,
address, phone number, etc.) are OK - no risk for the personal
integrity there. Password files, I'm fairly certain, fall into this
category.


It should be admitted that this particular text as well as the Data
Inspection has been severely criticized as going to far in trying to
protect people. 






