In article <2o66sm$co0@panix.com>, Mark Eckenwiler <eck@panix.com> wrote:
>In <1994Apr9.051741.21218@news.media.mit.edu>, rnewman@media.mit.edu sez:
>>I do not understand these objections to putting FAQs on CD-ROMs.  If I
>>wrote a FAQ, I'd be thrilled to have the additional readership that
>>off-line CD-ROM access makes possible.   Why wouldn't you?
>
>If Random House (to choose a random example) published selected FAQs
>in a book selling for $29.95

But we're not talking about a book.

We're talking about a CD, with FAQ's left in their original format.

The only problem I see is if the CD publisher uses editorial judgement
in selecting FAQ's.  If the CD publisher takes ALL current FAQ's posted
to (for example) news.answers and puts them on CD, I see no reason why
they need to ask for permission.  In the latter case it is simply an
extension of any standard Usenet feed or FTP mirror, and the CD
publisher is acting on good faith.

This flamewar would all be solved if the news.answers team only
accepted FAQ's that were released under the following license:

* All articles posted are copyrighted by the poster, unless otherwise
  stated.
* These articles may be copied and modified for personal use.

* Any copying or redistribution of articles posted to news.answers for
  other use must be done in the newsgroup's entirety, all in their
  original format.  These articles many not be individually or
  selectively copied or redistributed for other uses except by
  permission of the copyright holder(s), unless otherwise stated.
  Redistribution into other media must make reasonable efforts to
  maintain current copies of these articles, and not redistribute any
  peridic posting no longer under distribution.  (as listed under "List
  of Periodic Informational Postings")


Basically the intent is, if you are providing a CD which contains a
mirror of news.answers or rtfm.mit.edu:/pub/usenet, for example, that's
fine.  You don't need anyone's permission since that is "fair use".
(since it mirrors current behavior of Usenet)  But, if you use editorial
judgement, or otherwise exclude certain articles based on content, then
it is not "fair use", since Usenet operates on an all-or-nothing
basis.

