Subject: edupage-03.27.94
[...]
ENCRYPTION DEBATE
The Clinton Administration has underestimated the extent to which high-tech
and privacy groups oppose the Clipper and Tessara chips -- encryption
technologies that would allow federal agencies with court orders to
eavesdrop on data and voice communications. At the recent conference on
Computers, Freedom and Privacy, an Administration spokesman asked: "Do you
really want to live in a world where law enforcement can not do its job
because of the need for privacy?"  Pressed to explain how crooks and
terrorists smart enough to use encryption could be stupid enough to use the
government's own encryption standards, the spokesman insisted: "You
shouldn't overestimate the I.Q. of crooks." (New York Times 3/26/94 p.19)

SOFTWARE PATENT OVERTURNED
A broad software patent awarded to Compton New Media last August has been
overturned by the U.S. Patent and Trademark Office in the wake of protests
by software and computer companies. The patent would have given Compton's
parent company, Tribune Co., a monopoly on a technique essential to
retrieving combined sound, images and text. "The patent was so breathtaking
in its sweep that for people in the industry it was like trying to patent
sunlight," said one industry observer. (Wall Street Journal 3/25/94 B2)

[...]
VIRTUAL REAL ESTATE MARKET
No longer seen as a scarce resource, the broadcast spectrum now has
abundant new capacity, thanks to technological advances. Some experts think
the increased capacity will be matched by rising demand, but others say
that speculation over the spectrum is no different from real estate
speculation and is bound to collapse. (New York Times 3/27/94 Sec.4 p.6)

