nu7i@indirect.com (Darrell Shandrow) writes:

>Hello.  Well, I agree that he should not be accused for the actions of 
>others on his system.  However, there are aligations that he participated 
>directly in this pirating when he participated in the creation of some of 
>the readme files requesting software such as WordPerfect 6 and Excel. 

If you read the article in the MIT newspaper _The_Tech_, available on the web
at http://the-tech.mit.edu/, it seems to me that the MIT authorities were
not aware that the student workstations in question were being administered 
as a ftp site, and in fact the student who is charged with running the 
pirate ftp site was apparently *not* given permission to run an ftp site at
all on those systems.  I infer this from the comments on how the site was 
discovered: a student noticed unusual disk activity on one of the workstations
and reported it, and the site was discovered.  If the site had been officially 
sanctioned as a non-pirate ftp site, I would expect that the disk activity
would not have come as a suprise to the powers that be.

I should say that I cannot quote from the article because the tech site isn't
responding to me at the moment.  I have been trying to reconcile the two very 
different presentations by the defense lawyer on one side and the story as 
reported in The Tech on the other.  The defense statement implies that the
student was a sysadmin who was administrating a legit BBS, not filtering 
uploads, and is being put upon.  The other info implies, to me, that the 
was running an illicit FTP site on the school workstations without consent
of the school (itself possibly a federal crime) and was encouraging, by name, 
upload of commercial software.

So, here are the questions I would love to have answers to:

1) Was the student given permission to perform system tasks on the 
   workstations in question?  I mean, was he given access or did 
   he hack himself some room?

2) Was the student given permission to set up and administrate an 
   FTP site on said workstations?

-Ken


