A bit more info about FSP.  Please note that none of this is official in any
way, these are just personal observations on the state of the net.

	FSP is a clientless protocol, instead, it uses lots of little
binaries like MH, for different things, like listing files, etc...  FSP
servers can be set up on any machine without permission of the machine
owner, as the single server program doesn't spawn off anything else.  Also,
most FSP servers limit the amount of data transmitted, so it's not detected
as easily.  

	Why is it used for Warez etc?  Well, you can set up the server on a
machine and have it run in the background, under a random name.  It doesn't
spawn off any processes, and doesn't take up too much bandwidth (as it
limits how much data it sends to some reasonable rate), making it harder to
detect and easier on the machines.  And you don't need to be able to do
anything other then leave background processes running on a given machine.

	It is useful for other things, as some large FTP sites also allow
FSP (Wuarchive comes to mind), and in such cases, it will often still allow
FSP connections even when FTP doesn't allow any more, allowing a person to
get files (albeit slowly) even during peak hours on the server.

