In article <2o856t$iot@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu> kh13096@eehpx12 (Kenneth Lewis Hamer) writes:
[some questions]

	Disclaimer: I have no direct knowledge of the case, nor am I 
a lawyer. However, I am very familiar with the MIT system.
	Unsurprisingly, there are some errors in the mass media reports.
In a nutshell, what he was running was not a classic BBS, but an FSP
(FSP, not FTP) site. This was done on one of the "public" computer
workstations available for general use. His modifications of the machine
were a clear violation of the relevant computing policy. But it is much,
much less evident that they are literally a Federal case.

	To answer the specific questions:

>1) Was the student given permission to perform system tasks on the 
>   workstations in question?  I mean, was he given access or did 
>   he hack himself some room?

	No, he was not given permission. Only a few people have such
system level permission. MIT has a very open system where ordinary users
have the technical ability to modify machines, but they are only
supposed to use this ability to fix minor local system problems. To do
otherwise is a violation of the "Rules of Use". The exact phrasing is:

	* You should not attempt to interfere with or alter the
	integrity of the system at large, as by any of the following:
	...
        	- reconfiguration of workstations

	He hacked himself some space. The particular machine he was
using has ~ 200 Megs of unused local disk space (and it is not unique,
there are 54 others just like it in that lab - MIT is VERY computer-rich).

>2) Was the student given permission to set up and administrate an 
>   FTP site on said workstations?

	Absolutely not. He was using a machine of a type which are never
intended to be used as FTP, FSP or BBS servers.

	I think people (most especially D* S*) are missing an important
element of the case. If the facts are as alleged, he clearly did violate
computing policy. The defense argument does not dispute this. They say
*HE DID NOT COMMIT THE CRIME HE IS CHARGED WITH*. He is not charged by
the Federal government with the crime of misusing MIT computers (that is
not, in itself, a Federal felony yet, or A LOT of students are guilty), or
with copyright violation. He is charged under "18 U.S.C. S 371 - Conspiracy".

	The defense says : "David LaMacchia's conduct was not in
violation of the criminal law". They don't say he was authorized to do
what he did. They don't say copyright violation didn't occur on the site.
Rather, they say he didn't profit by it, and it is "not at all clear"
that the creation of such a site where violation occurs is a crime.

	In essence, I think all sides stipulate that an FSP site was
created, and I'm quite sure this can be established as a violation of
MIT policy. The divergences occur after this. The government alleges
that it he did "devise and execute a scheme and artifice to defraud ...".
The evident presented for this intent is extremely thin - one message,
PURPORTED to be from him, requesting commercial software, and a lot of
factoids used to give a furtive or conspiratorial flavor (communicating
with PGP? Ack!). Nowhere does the government supply evidence that the
site was intended to be a pirate one, indeed a complete reading of the
indictment shows no statements from the accused to that effect.

	Without more evidence, my opinion is that the government's
case is extremely weak in establishing that he committed the crime in
the indictment (NOT that he was a bad boy), and the defense has an
excellent argument. Basically the defense can argue that he was just
playing around with FSP. It was against school rules, and this
explains all the hiding behavior in the indictment. Note this is far
from the first case of people misusing workstations at MIT (those
~ 200 Meg disks and fast CPU's are very tempting). They can even admit
he did request the commercial stuff, but claim it was incidental. In a
sentence, argue the FSP site was a (rule-breaking) exploration, it was not
*created* to commit piracy, it was not a "conspiracy and scheme to defraud".

	This is where I believe, on the evidence presented so far, the
defense warrants our assistance and support. My basic take on the case
is that it is a tremendous overkill. The claims of damage seem
ridiculously inflated, and the student's behavior could probably have
been stopped very easily by a comment from the systems staff. This
might have warranted a formal reprimand, or even probation, from the
disciplinary process, but that's a far cry from a criminal trial. I
wonder if the prosecutors took it on for other elements, such as
establishing some precedent that use of cryptography can be taken to
infer conspiracy (that part about PGP bears VERY close watching).
There is also the disturbing overall trend of transmuting relatively
minor alleged civil-law illegalities into criminal-law prosecutions.

	In short, this case won't be another Steve Jackson Games,
where the defendants were innocent victims of an incredible paranoia
(a "hacker-hunt"). Rather, it _appears_ more along the lines of bad RICO
lawsuits, where people *may* have done something wrong, but the
charges and penalties they face are massively disproportionate to
their apparent culpability. It is of course much harder to garner
support in these cases, since distinction of being "guilty of
crimes" and "guilty as charged" tends to get lost - few people are
willing to defend "criminals". I conjecture we will see a lot of writing
confusing the two concepts in the discussion of this case.

--
