As the sole author of one of the FAQs in question, which includes a
very explicit copyright notice (plus generous permission statement),
my position is this:

 I expect commercial enterprises to ask my permission to
 publish my FAQ.  That is my right.  Even if I never deny
 permission to anyone, it is still my right to do so.
 (If I were the maintainer of a compilation FAQ, I would be
 very careful to comply with the wishes of the contributors,
 just as is done with any publication that has contributing
 authors.  This issue is largely irrelvant to the problem of
 what to do about distribution via CD.)

 I maintain and distribute my FAQ as a public service, and
 as such I am interested in seeing that the public does not
 get ripped off by unscrupulous commercial enterprises that
 charge an order of magnitude too much for access to my work.

 I find a CD costing $45 ($39.95 plus $5 shipping) to be
 *exorbitant*;  the total production costs don't come
 anywhere near $45 per copy.

 I find a CD costing $45 and not including any value-added
 feature whatsoever to be *antithetical* to the spirit of
 Usenet.  In the Walnut Creek CD blurb there was no mention
 of cross-referencing, no full-text search tool.  Walnut
 Creek can't even bost the added value of a compilation,
 since the FAQs are already compiled:  in news.answers.
 And they certainly don't provide any other service, unlike
 Internet access providers (an example offered as similar).

 I will *gladly* give permission to distribute my FAQ on CD
 to anyone who (1) asks first and (2) charges what I consider
 to be a reasonable price.  A reasonably priced CD will get
 my FAQ into the hands of far more people.

 I do not want to see the low-budget, low-profile efforts of
 the many wonderful people who contribute *free* services to
 be swamped by the "sharks" who are rushing in to make a
 killing.  That will be the death of the spirit that I hear
 praised by so many advocates of the "CD is no different"
 position.  People who do things for free get (by definition)
 very little reward:  one reward I *expect* is due respect
 for whatever modest restrictions I place on the use of my FAQ.

 Those who wish to place no restrictions whatsoever on the
 distribution of their documents are *welcome* to do so.  But
 I see no good reason why they should expect *me* to do the
 same.

By the way, information in the public domain may not be sold for profit.
It is generally considered unethical and mean-spirited to package public
domain materials (such as most freeware on diskette) and charge a high
price for the "service".

